                        GCompris HACKING

Official repository
-------------------
The official repository is hosted on KDE Invent:

    https://invent.kde.org/education/gcompris

Create a KDE Identity account, fork the project on KDE Invent, push a topic
branch to your fork, and open a merge request against the official repository.
Regular contributors can request developer access and work directly on KDE
repositories.


Source code from GitHub
-----------------------
The GitHub repository is a mirror. It is useful for browsing and cloning, but
KDE Invent is the preferred place for merge requests.

    git clone https://github.com/gcompris/GCompris-qt.git

If you want to have a personal fork, in order to commit occasional patches,
fork it on GitHub then clone your fork

    git clone https://github.com/<username>/GCompris-qt.git

When asking for a pull request, create a new topic branch and commit your
changes there then open a pull request to gcompris-qt repository on GitHub.

See GitHub's documentation for more information.

    https://help.github.com/categories/collaborating/


Dependencies
------------
In order to be able to compile GCompris the following Qt6 dependencies need to
be installed (version 6.5.0 minimum)

    Qt 6 Development Defaults
    Qt 6 qmake
    Qt Creator
    Qt Linguist Tools

    Qt Core
    Qt Gui
    Qt Multimedia
    Qt Multimedia QML Module
    Qt Network
    Qt Image Formats
    Qt Qml
    Qt Quick
    Qt Sensors
    Qt Svg
    Qt Charts (qml plugin)

CMake must be installed to compile GCompris:

    sudo apt install cmake

By default, translations are also built so gettext package must be installed to compile GCompris (to ignore translation files, run cmake with `-DSKIP_TRANSLATIONS=ON`:

    sudo apt install gettext
    
Installing dependencies on a Debian based systems can be done like this:

    sudo apt install libssl-dev libqt6svg6-dev \
                     qt6-multimedia-dev qt6-tools-dev qt6-tools-dev-tools \
                     libqt6sensors6-dev libqt6svg6-dev \
                     linguist-qt6 qt6-l10n-tools \
                     qt6-declarative6-dev qml6-module-qtcharts \
                     libqt6charts6-dev \
                     qml6-module-qtcore qml6-module-qtquick-controls \
                     qml6-module-qtmultimedia qml6-module-qtqml \
                     qml6-module-qtqml-workerscript qml6-module-qtquick \
                     qml6-module-qtquick-window qt6-image-formats-plugins \
                     qml6-module-qtquick-templates


To build for Android, Qt 6 AndroidExtras also needs to be installed.

If building from a .tar.gz or .zip source distribution the externals may
need to be added.

    cd GCompris-qt-*
    git clone https://github.com/qml-box2d/qml-box2d.git external/qml-box2d/


Build
-----
Start QtCreator and select Open Project and open CMakeLists.txt in the
gcompris-qt root directory. Follow the wizard instructions.

Use the buttons on the lower left side to build, run, and debug GCompris.

An alternative building process is to use cmake in command-line directly.

    cd GCompris-qt-*
    mkdir build
    cd build
    cmake ..
    make
    ./bin/gcompris-qt

On Windows, the build is done using Ninja, MinGW and OpenSSL provided by Qt Maintenance tool (under Developer and Designer Tools). Make sure you added them to the PATH (export PATH=$PATH:/c/Qt/Tools/mingw1120_64/bin:/c/Qt/Tools/Ninja):

    cd GCompris-qt-*
    mkdir build
    cd build
    cmake -G "Ninja" -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/c/Qt/6.5.3/mingw_64/ -DOPENSSL_ROOT_DIR=/c/Qt/Tools/OpenSSLv3/Win_x64/ ..
    cmake --build . -j 4 --config Release --target BuildTranslations
    cmake --build . -j 4 --config Release
    cpack -C Release .


For Android, install Qt with the Android flavor you want from Qt Maintenance tool and run the script in tools to build for armeabi-v7a (./tools/android_build_all_apk.sh) or arm64-v8a (./tools/android_build_all_apk_64.sh). It takes the Qt root folder as argument (~/Qt/6.5.3/ for example).

macOS
-----
Install the build tools and Qt 6 libraries. With Homebrew:

    brew install cmake ninja qt pkgconf openssl@3

Configure a local standalone build with the Homebrew Qt prefix. GCompris uses
AAC audio assets on Apple by default; use OGG if the local environment does not
provide the conversion tools required for AAC packaging.

    cmake -S . -B build-macos -G Ninja \
          -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/homebrew \
          -DOPENSSL_ROOT_DIR=/opt/homebrew/opt/openssl@3 \
          -DCOMPRESSED_AUDIO=ogg
    cmake --build build-macos --parallel
    cmake --install build-macos --prefix /Applications

If building from a source archive instead of a full git checkout, make sure the
external qml-box2d dependency exists:

    git clone https://github.com/qml-box2d/qml-box2d.git external/qml-box2d/

The standalone bundle must contain Qt's `tls` plugins and the OpenSSL libraries
used by Qt. If updating external assets fails with
`Download error (code: 99): TLS initialization failed`, check that the installed
bundle contains and can load the Qt TLS backend, for example:

    ls /Applications/gcompris-qt.app/Contents/Plugins/tls
    otool -L /Applications/gcompris-qt.app/Contents/Plugins/tls/libqopensslbackend.dylib

For local testing on macOS, ad-hoc sign the installed bundle after installation.
Sign nested Mach-O files first, then the bundle, otherwise macOS can terminate
the app or a Qt plugin with `Code Signature Invalid`.

    chmod -R u+w /Applications/gcompris-qt.app
    xattr -cr /Applications/gcompris-qt.app
    find /Applications/gcompris-qt.app -type f -print0 | while IFS= read -r -d '' file; do
        if file -b "$file" | grep -q "Mach-O" && [ "$file" != "/Applications/gcompris-qt.app/Contents/MacOS/gcompris-qt" ]; then
            codesign --force --sign - "$file"
        fi
    done
    codesign --force --deep --sign - /Applications/gcompris-qt.app
    codesign --verify --deep --strict --verbose=2 /Applications/gcompris-qt.app

Ad-hoc signing is only for a local developer build. Public macOS downloads need
a Developer ID signature and Apple notarization, and should be produced through
KDE release infrastructure. KDE's Craft tooling is the usual cross-platform
packaging entry point for KDE applications and can create macOS DMG packages.

